Core Architecture & Physical Infrastructure
Define business goals, bandwidth requirements, and high-availability targets.
Design the high-level network topology covering Campus, Data Center, and WAN.
Formulate a standardized IP addressing schema (IPv4/IPv6 subnets).
Create a comprehensive VLAN ID allocation matrix for all network segments.
Standardize hardware specifications for Layer 2 switches, Nexus, SD-WAN, WAPs, and SASE.
Conduct site surveys for rack space, power availability, and cooling capacity.
Verify circuit availability and coordinate with internet service providers (ISPs).
Order hardware, licenses, and optical modules based on the approved design.
Inspect delivered equipment for physical damage and verify serial numbers.
Inventory all assets in the Configuration Management Database (CMDB).
Mount Layer 2 switches, Nexus switches, and SD-WAN routers into designated racks.
Run structured copper and fiber cabling adhering to color-code standards.
Label all cables, ports, power chords, and equipment chassis clearly.
Connect redundant power supplies to separate Power Distribution Units (PDUs).
Perform initial power-on self-tests (POST) on all physical devices.

Layer 2 Switch Deployment
Establish out-of-band (OOB) management access via console ports.
Upgrade switch firmware to the vendor-approved stable baseline image.
Configure basic system settings including hostnames, banners, and time zones.
Harden device access using SSH, complex local accounts, and password encryption.
Configure AAA (Authentication, Authorization, and Accounting) via TACACS+ or RADIUS.
Set up Management VLANs and assign static IP addresses to switches.
Create and name data, voice, guest, IoT, and quarantine VLANs globally.
Configure access ports with appropriate VLAN assignments and descriptions.
Implement 802.1Q trunking on links connecting upstream distribution switches.
Configure Multiple Spanning Tree Protocol (MSTP) or Rapid Per-VLAN Spanning Tree (RPVST+).
Optimize Spanning Tree with PortFast and BPDU Guard on all edge ports.
Configure Link Aggregation Control Protocol (LACP) EtherChannels for high bandwidth.
Enable Loop Guard and Root Guard to prevent Spanning Tree topology issues.
Implement Layer 2 security features like DHCP Snooping and Dynamic ARP Inspection (DAI).
Enable Port Security with MAC address limits on sensitive access ports.
Configure LLDP and CDP for neighbor discovery and asset tracking.
Set up SNMPv3 or NetConf for network monitoring system integration.

Data Center Nexus Switch 
Initialize Cisco Nexus switches in Nx-OS mode or ACI mode based on design.
Enable required feature sets including LACP, VPC, LLDP, and UDLD.
Configure the Management VRF and dedicated out-of-band management interfaces.
Set up Virtual Port Channels (vPC) domain, peer-keepalive link, and peer-link.
Configure vPC member ports toward downstream servers and Layer 2 switches.
Deploy a Spine-and-Leaf topology using VXLAN EVPN for data center fabric fabric.
Configure Underlay routing using Open Shortest Path First (OSPF) or BGP.
Define VXLAN Network Identifiers (VNIs) and Map them to specific VLANs.
Configure Network Virtualization Edge (NVE) interfaces for VTEP peers.
Implement Anycast Gateways on Leaf switches for seamless virtual machine migration.
Configure Multi-Protocol BGP (MP-BGP) EVPN control plane for MAC/IP route advertisement.
Set up Jumbo Frames by adjusting Maximum Transmission Units (MTU) to 9216 fabric-wide.
Implement Quality of Service (QoS) classes for storage, data, and management traffic.
Integrate hardware security policies using Router ACLs and Port ACLs.
Configure Nexus Smart Licensing and register switches to the Cisco Smart Account.

SD-WAN Router
Initialize the SD-WAN controllers (vManage/Manager, vSmart/Validator, vBond/Bond).
Generate and install security certificates across all SD-WAN controllers.
Define the global SD-WAN fabric organization name and control parameters.
Configure system properties on edge routers including System IP, Site ID, and Organization.
Set up VPN 0 for WAN Transport connections (Internet, MPLS, 4G/5G).
Configure VPN 512 for dedicated out-of-band management access.
Set up Service-Side VPNs to route internal corporate traffic.
Create SD-WAN localized policies for basic routing protocols like OSPF and BGP.
Build centralized control policies to govern topology, routing, and service chaining.
Create Application-Aware Routing (AAR) policies based on SLA metrics (loss, latency, jitter).
Deploy Direct Internet Access (DIA) policies for trusted cloud SaaS applications.
Configure localized data policies for access control lists and QoS queuing.
Establish secure IPSec/DTLS control connections between edge routers and controllers.
Validate BFD (Bidirectional Forwarding Detection) sessions across all WAN transports.

Wireless Access Point (WAP) 
Install and configure central Wireless LAN Controllers (WLCs) in high-availability pairs.
Set up CAPWAP or Catalyst Center controllers for access point provisioning.
Configure DHCP option 43/120 to provide WLC IP addresses to booting WAPs.
Mount Access Points in surveyed locations adhering to spatial layout guidelines.
Connect WAPs to PoE+ (Power over Ethernet) enabled Layer 2 switch ports.
Verify AP enrollment, firmware synchronization, and successful controller registration.
Create Service Set Identifiers (SSIDs) for Corporate, Guest, and IoT segments.
Configure 802.1X enterprise authentication using EAP-TLS/PEAP for Corporate SSIDs.
Set up a secure Captive Portal with web-auth for the Guest wireless network.
Configure RF Profiles adjusting channel width, transmit power, and data rates.
Enable CleanAir or equivalent features for real-time radio frequency interference mitigation.
Configure wireless Quality of Service (QoS) mappings to prioritize voice and video.
Implement Rogue AP detection and prevention mechanisms to secure the airwaves.

Zero Touch Provisioning (ZTP) 
Set up a local ZTP server infrastructure including DHCP, TFTP, HTTP, and DNS.
Configure DHCP server scopes with Option 66, Option 67, or vendor-specific options.
Create device baseline configuration templates within the orchestration engine.
Map device MAC addresses or serial numbers to their specific configuration files.
Connect the unconfigured network device to the ZTP-enabled network port.
Boot the device to trigger an automatic DHCP request for ZTP server information.
Download the designated software image and baseline configuration file automatically.
Monitor the automated reboot and verify successful application of settings via ZTP logs.

Network Automation
Establish a centralized automation workstation with Python, Ansible, and Git installed.
Build a secure Git repository to store, version, and collaborate on infrastructure code.
Construct structured YAML Ansible inventories containing target device variables.
Develop Ansible Playbooks or Python scripts to push standard configurations at scale.
Implement API-driven workflows utilizing Netconf, Restconf, or vendor-specific REST APIs.
Set up a CI/CD pipeline to automatically test, validate, and deploy changes safely.
